[{"key":"concurrency/sqlite-reader-writer-overlap","assignment":"concurrency","row":{"id":"sqlite-reader-writer-overlap","slot":"concurrency","finding":"SQLite WAL documents concurrent reading and writing: readers do not block writers and a writer does not block readers.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html","title":"Write-Ahead Logging","passage":"WAL provides more concurrency as readers do not block writers and a writer does not block readers. Reading and writing can proceed concurrently.","locator":"Advantages, introductory WAL section","origin":"SQLite official documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation","responseId":"mtqvh49r38e0zl"}],"counterevidence":"The same retrieved page says this is \"mostly true\" and that some WAL-mode queries can return SQLITE_BUSY; applications should be prepared for that circumstance.","method":"Applies when the database is operating in SQLite WAL mode. This is documented locking behavior, not a throughput measurement.","comparability":"Directly comparable only to reader/writer overlap. It does not establish behavior for overlapping conflicting write transactions.","confidence":"High for the stated reader/writer behavior because the exact current official WAL passage was retrieved.","disposition":"retain"},"eligibleForReview":true,"receiptPath":"/home/balauru/.pi-profiles/fabric/tmp/fabric-research-rework-review/behavior-evaluator-final/live-run-20260328-a-cde/worker-concurrency.json","checked":false},{"key":"concurrency/postgres-reader-writer-overlap","assignment":"concurrency","row":{"id":"postgres-reader-writer-overlap","slot":"concurrency","finding":"PostgreSQL documents that MVCC read locks do not conflict with write locks, so reading never blocks writing and writing never blocks reading.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc-intro.html","title":"13.1. Introduction","passage":"The main advantage of using the MVCC model of concurrency control rather than locking is that in MVCC locks acquired for querying (reading) data do not conflict with locks acquired for writing data, and so reading never blocks writing and writing never blocks reading.","locator":"MVCC introduction, paragraph 2","origin":"PostgreSQL official documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation","responseId":"mtqvh49r38e0zl"}],"method":"Applies to PostgreSQL's documented MVCC behavior. This is not a measurement and does not define outcomes when writers contend for the same row.","comparability":"Comparable to SQLite only for reader/writer overlap; write/write conflict handling was not extracted as an exact passage in this stream.","confidence":"High for the stated reader/writer behavior because the exact current official MVCC passage was retrieved.","disposition":"retain"},"eligibleForReview":true,"receiptPath":"/home/balauru/.pi-profiles/fabric/tmp/fabric-research-rework-review/behavior-evaluator-final/live-run-20260328-a-cde/worker-concurrency.json","checked":false},{"key":"concurrency/sqlite-wal-deployment-boundary","assignment":"concurrency","row":{"id":"sqlite-wal-deployment-boundary","slot":"deployment","finding":"SQLite WAL requires every process using the database to be on the same host; it does not work over a network filesystem.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html","title":"Write-Ahead Logging","passage":"All processes using a database must be on the same host computer; WAL does not work over a network filesystem. This is because WAL requires all processes to share a small amount of memory and processes on separate host machines obviously cannot share memory with each other.","locator":"Disadvantages, introductory WAL section","origin":"SQLite official documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation","responseId":"mtqvh49r38e0zl"}],"method":"Applies to SQLite in WAL mode where processes access the database directly.","comparability":"This is a file-access deployment constraint, unlike PostgreSQL's client/server network interface.","confidence":"High because the exact current official WAL limitation was retrieved.","disposition":"retain"},"eligibleForReview":true,"receiptPath":"/home/balauru/.pi-profiles/fabric/tmp/fabric-research-rework-review/behavior-evaluator-final/live-run-20260328-a-cde/worker-concurrency.json","checked":false},{"key":"concurrency/postgres-client-server-deployment","assignment":"concurrency","row":{"id":"postgres-client-server-deployment","slot":"deployment","finding":"PostgreSQL documents a client/server model: server and client may be on different hosts over TCP/IP, and the server can handle multiple concurrent client connections.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-arch.html","title":"1.2. Architectural Fundamentals","passage":"As is typical of client/server applications, the client and the server can be on different hosts. In that case they communicate over a TCP/IP network connection.","locator":"Architectural Fundamentals, client/server description","origin":"PostgreSQL official documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation","responseId":"mtqvh49r38e0zl"},{"url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-arch.html","title":"1.2. Architectural Fundamentals","passage":"The PostgreSQL server can handle multiple concurrent connections from clients.","locator":"Architectural Fundamentals, connection handling","origin":"PostgreSQL official documentation","sourceType":"Primary documentation","responseId":"mtqvh49r38e0zl"}],"method":"Applies to PostgreSQL's documented client/server architecture.","comparability":"This establishes networked client access, not a throughput claim or a configuration-independent limit on connections.","confidence":"High for the architectural facts because exact current official passages were retrieved.","disposition":"retain"},"eligibleForReview":true,"receiptPath":"/home/balauru/.pi-profiles/fabric/tmp/fabric-research-rework-review/behavior-evaluator-final/live-run-20260328-a-cde/worker-concurrency.json","checked":false},{"key":"backups/sqlite-online-backup","assignment":"backups","row":{"id":"sqlite-online-backup","slot":"backup","finding":"SQLite's Online Backup API permits a live source database to be backed up while other connections read and write it; the destination remains in a write transaction for the backup duration.","status":"documented_fact","support":[{"url":"https://www.sqlite.org/c3ref/backup_finish.html","title":"Online Backup API.","passage":"SQLite holds a write transaction open on the destination database file for the duration of the backup operation. The source database is read-locked only while it is being read; it is not locked continuously for the entire backup operation. Thus, the backup may be performed on a live source database without preventing other database connections from reading or writing to the source database while the backup is underway.","locator":"Online Backup API overview","sourceType":"Primary documentation","responseId":"mtqvh286k8nqsk"}],"method":"Documented API locking semantics, not a workload measurement. Applies to backups made through SQLite's Online Backup API.","comparability":"This is a database-file/API backup mechanism, whereas PostgreSQL pg_basebackup is a physical cluster backup; no throughput comparison follows.","confidence":"High for the stated API behavior because the decisive current primary documentation passage was retrieved.","disposition":"retain"},"eligibleForReview":true,"receiptPath":"/home/balauru/.pi-profiles/fabric/tmp/fabric-research-rework-review/behavior-evaluator-final/live-run-20260328-a-cde/worker-backups.json","checked":false},{"key":"backups/sqlite-wal-writer-limit","assignment":"backups","row":{"id":"sqlite-wal-writer-limit","slot":"operational-risk","finding":"SQLite WAL mode still serializes writes: one WAL file permits only one writer at a time, so it does not make overlapping write